Subject: Fix a stack leak, a capture-flag leak and unchecked slot operands in the register VM

What this does
  Six fixes found by an audit of the register VM and the bytecode reader.

  1. A loop's backward branch that drains the deferred reference-count table
     now reaches the drain as a guaranteed tail call. It was a plain call, so
     every drain left one interpreter frame on the native stack until the
     function returned. Optimized builds happened to turn it into a jump;
     unoptimized and sanitizer builds did not, and a long loop that
     accumulates into an object property overflowed the native stack.

  2. Loading a function from bytecode no longer clears its "an argument is
     captured" flag. The variable definitions are read first and set the flag
     for a parameter an inner closure captures; the register program decode
     then assigned the flag from the program alone, which cannot see that
     capture. The function then borrowed its caller's argument window while a
     closure cell referenced it, and the reference counts no longer balanced:
     an assertion in debug builds, a leak in release builds.

  3. Every register opcode operand that indexes a frame's locals, arguments,
     closure references or registers is now bounded when bytecode is loaded.
     Twelve opcodes were missing from the check, among them get_local_field,
     put_local_xfer, local_rmw_imm, slot_get, slot_put, using_dispose and the
     branch_cmp_*_loc forms, and their handlers index without checking.
     using_dispose also reads the slot after its operand, so that one must be
     in range too. The check is one function shared by the wire decoder and
     the ahead-of-time verifier, which had none of these checks at all.

  4. Reading a truncated BigInt no longer leaks the partly filled value.

  5. A stack-shaping instruction with more results than the baseline tier's
     fixed copy arrays hold is refused instead of being truncated on record
     and overrun on expansion. Today's shapers produce at most six, so this
     only keeps a future one from overflowing the arrays. The unused is_perm
     field is gone.

  6. Whether a light frame may run an opcode is computed per opcode instead
     of from a static table filled on first use with no synchronization, which
     two runtimes on different threads could race on.

Why we need it
  1 is a hard crash in any build the optimizer does not rescue. 2 is a leak in
  shipping builds for any function whose captured parameter is read through a
  computed property access with a side-effecting key. 3 lets malformed or
  corrupted bytecode read and write outside a frame. 4 is a leak on malformed
  input. 5 and 6 are latent.

What it adds
  regvm_slot_operands_ok()  - says whether every slot operand of one register
                              instruction is in range for its function.
  REGVM_SHAPE_MAX           - the most results one stack shaper may produce.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No. The format is unchanged; the reader only refuses more malformed input.

Notes
  The argument-capture flag now combines with the variable definitions the way
  the ahead-of-time load path already did.

